  These aren't hypothetical for long-lived gateway processes — NTP slew and step corrections are routine.

  The underlying need — correlating a limiter decision with a wall-clock log line — is real, but it doesn't require
  changing the window arithmetic. Two options that keep monotonic as the window clock:

  - Log [38;5;153mtime.time()[39m at the call site when the limiter's decision is recorded. The log line gets a wall-clock stamp; the
    window math stays monotonic.
  - Store a pair per event, [38;5;153m(monotonic, wall)[39m, and expire on the monotonic value while exposing the wall value for
    diagnostics. Costs memory and complexity, so only worth it if something actually reads back the stored stamps.

  Do you want option 1, option 2, or does the reviewer have a scenario I'm not seeing where the window itself must be
  wall-clock-based?

  [1m3. Pluggable storage-backend interface — pushing back (YAGNI).[0m

  I grepped the repo: no Redis anywhere, no second backend, no config that selects one, no caller outside [38;5;153mtests/[39m.
  [38;5;153mdependencies = [][39m in [38;5;153mpyproject.toml[39m. This is a 27-line class with one in-memory implementation and a single
  hypothetical future consumer.

  Building the abstraction now costs more than deferring it, and is likely to be the wrong abstraction: an in-process
  deque and Redis have genuinely different semantics. The deque version is single-process and non-atomic; a correct
  Redis sliding window needs an atomic [38;5;153mZREMRANGEBYSCORE[39m + [38;5;153mZCARD[39m + [38;5;153mZADD[39m (a Lua script or [38;5;153mMULTI[39m), plus key TTLs, plus a
  failure policy when Redis is unreachable — fail-open or fail-closed. An interface designed against the deque today
  won't express any of that, so the Redis work would reshape it anyway. Meanwhile the extra indirection is carried by
  every reader in the interim.

  Extracting the interface when the second implementation actually lands is a mechanical refactor on a class this small
  — the deque touches three lines.

  If there's a committed plan to move to Redis (a ticket, a scaling deadline), tell me and I'll design the interface
  against the Redis semantics rather than the deque, since that's the constraint that matters. Otherwise I'd leave it.

  Net: item 1 applied, items 2 and 3 awaiting your call.

